= The April Crisis

On 3 May the newspapers published a note by Foreign Minister Milyukov to the Allies, promising that Russia would fight on to a decisive victory and honour all the tsarist treaties. Armed soldiers and workers poured into the streets of Petrograd, carrying banners reading "Down with Milyukov". Counter-demonstrations by the middle classes appeared the next day. General Kornilov, commander of the Petrograd garrison, offered to clear the streets by force, and Soviet leaders refused.

The cabinet is in crisis. The Soviet must decide whether to bring the government down, or to enter it.

- @press: Press the government to repudiate the note, and restrain the crowds.
- @control: Withdraw the Soviet's support and hold to the control role.
- @reshuffle: Demand a coalition government, with Milyukov out.

@press
on-arrival: {!
RO.add(Q, {rel_kad: -4, soviet_democracy: 1, right_threat: 1});
RO.boost(Q, {workers: 1, soldiers: 1});
RO.fac(Q, 'defencist', 1, 0);
!}

Milyukov explained that the note had been misunderstood. The crowds went home, but the cabinet is weakened.

@control
on-arrival: {!
Q.refused_coalition = 1;
RO.add(Q, {rel_kad: -6, right_threat: 2, soviet_democracy: 1});
RO.fac(Q, 'intl', 3, -3);
RO.fac(Q, 'defencist', -2, 5);
!}

The Soviet will remain a body of supervision. The government grows weaker with every week.

@reshuffle
on-arrival: {!
Q.demanded_coalition = 1;
RO.add(Q, {rel_kad: -2, rel_ally: 3});
RO.fac(Q, 'defencist', 3, 0);
RO.fac(Q, 'intl', -2, 6);
!}

A coalition in which the Soviet parties share ministries with the liberals is the only escape from dual power, and the Soviet has endorsed it.
